          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Algebraic K-theory is about natural constructions of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theories"} cohomology theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
            data such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            and various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            refinements of these.
          %p
            From a modern perspective, the algebraic K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{K}(R)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            is simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} ∞-group completion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+vector+bundles"} algebraic vector bundles
            on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AsTheKTheoryOfAlgebraicVectorBundles"} below
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brave+new+rings"} brave new rings
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} E-∞ rings
          %p
            Historically, the
            %em algebraic K-theory
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            (what today is the “0th” algebraic K-theory group) was originally defined to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+modules"} projective modules
            (under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of modules). Under the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "module#RelationToVectorBundlesInIntroduction"} relation between modules and vector bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            then algebraic K-theory subsumes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            and also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#OnMonoidalStacks"} below
          %p
            There are canonical maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Pic
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_0(R)\to Pic(R)
            from the 0th algebraic K-theory of a ring to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_1(R)\to GL_1(R)
            from the first algebraic K-theory group of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
            which are given in components by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
            functor. This fact is sometimes used to motivate algebraic K-theory as a “generalization of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+algebra"} linear algebra
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/171369/381"} this MO discussion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regulator+of+a+number+field"} regulator of a number field
            relates to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson+regulators"} Beilinson regulators
            of algebraic K-theory.
          %p
            More generally, following the axiomatics of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology
            any
            %em algebraic K-theory
            should be given by a sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i
            from some suitable class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            of “algebraic nature” to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            these groups should arise as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+spectrum"} K-theory spectrum
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+Q-construction"} Quillen Q-construction
            defined on
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+exact+categories"} Quillen exact categories
            and more generally the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
            defined on
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+categories"} Waldhausen categories
          %p
            For more on the history of the subject see (
            %a{:href => "#Arlettaz04"}> Arlettaz 04
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Grayson13"} Grayson 13
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Algebraic+K-theory%2C+a+historical+perspective"} Algebraic K-theory, a historical perspective
          %p
            There are two ways to think of the traditional algebraic K-theory of a commutative ring more conceptually: on the one hand this construction is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequences"} short exact sequences
            in that category. This leads to the two modern ways of expressing and viewing algebraic K-theory:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong monoidal.
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
                or more generally of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal
                completion to an
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+%E2%88%9E-group"} abelian ∞-group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+spectrum"} connective spectrum
                optained by universally adjoining
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverses"} inverses
                to the symmetric monoidal operation – the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} ∞-group completion
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+permutative+category"} algebraic K-theory of a symmetric monoidal category
                and more generally that of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} algebraic K-theory of a symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %strong exact/stable.
                Analogously, inverting the addition operation expressed by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
                in an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
                or more generally in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
                yields the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory+of+a+stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} algebraic K-theory of a stable (∞,1)-category
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+Q-construction"} Quillen Q-construction
                and the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+motives"} non-commutative motives
          %p
            Here the second construction may be understood as first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+exact+sequence"} splitting
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
            and then applying the first construction to the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            monoidal structure. Typically the first construction here contains more information but is harder to compute, and vice versa (see also MO-discussion
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/98602/381"} here
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/102583/381"} here
          %p
            Both of these constructions produce a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            (hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} representing
            a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology+theory"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+spectrum"} K-theory spectrum
            – and the algebraic K-theory groups are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of that spectrum.
          %p
            The classical case of the algebraic K-theory of a commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a special case of this general concept of algebraic K-theory by either forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Mod(R), \oplus)
            and applying the
            = succeed "-completion" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+%E2%88%9E-group"} abelian ∞-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+chain+complexes"} stable (∞,1)-category of chain complexes
            of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
            to that. In both cases the result is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            whose degree-0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            is the ordinary algebraic K-theory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            as given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            and whose higher homotopy groups are its
            %em higher algebraic K-theory
            groups.
          %h2#constructions Constructions
          %h3#symmetric_monoidal_ktheory Symmetric monoidal K-theory
          %p
            For a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+subgroupoid"} maximal subgroupoid
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i C \subset C
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(i C)
              (a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+monoid"} topological monoid
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi B
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega B B (i C)
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} K-theory of a symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+permutative+category"} K-theory of a permutative category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+bipermutative+category"} K-theory of a bipermutative category
          %h3#quillen_qconstruction_for_exact_categories Quillen Q-construction (for exact categories)
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+exact+category"} Quillen exact category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(E)
            by applying
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+Q-construction"} Quillen Q-construction
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(E)
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi B
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega B Q(E)
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+Q-construction"} Quillen Q-construction
          %h3#waldhausen_construction_for_waldhausen_categories
            Waldhausen
            = succeed "-construction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\bullet
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+category"} Waldhausen category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, w C)
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mo •
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w S_\bullet C
              (a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+category"} simplicial category
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              (a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+set"} bisimplicial set
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              (a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              (a
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
          %p
            There is also a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} stable (infinity,1)-categories
            and, most generally, for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} Waldhausen (infinity,1)-categories
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Waldhausen+S-construction"} Waldhausen S-construction
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} K-theory of a stable (infinity,1)-category
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#for_rings For rings
          %p
            We recall several constructions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Weibel"} Weibel, IV.4.8, IV.4.11.1
          %h4#plus_construction Plus construction
          %p
            Given an associative unital
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi BGL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(R) = BGL(R)^+
            by taking
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+groups"} general linear groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi GL
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL_n(R)
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 0
            %li
              their
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi BGL
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi colim
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mi BGL
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BGL(R) = colim_n BGL_n(R)
            %li
              the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+plus+construction"} Quillen plus construction
          %h4#direct_sum_ktheory Direct sum K-theory
          %p
            Consider the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(X)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated"} finitely generated
            %span.newWikiWord
              projective
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/projective"} ?
            (right)
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+structure"} symmetric monoidal structure
            given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(R)
            may be described as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} K-theory of a symmetric monoidal (infinity,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(R)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi B
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(R) = \Omega B B (i P(X))
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i P(X)
            denotes the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+subgroupoid"} maximal subgroupoid
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Weibel"} Weibel, IV.4.8, IV.4.11.1
          %h4#exact_ktheory Exact K-theory
          %p
            Consider the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(X)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+generated"} finitely generated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective
            (right)
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
            and the K-theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(R)
            may be described via the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+Q-construction"} Quillen Q-construction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
