          %p
            from the algebraic K-theory spectrum to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Hochschild+homology"} topological Hochschild homology
            spectrum and factoring through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+cyclic+homology"} topological cyclic homology
            spectrum called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclotomic+trace"} cyclotomic trace
            which much like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            map for algebraic K-theory.
          %h3#comparison_map_and_relation_to_topological_ktheory Comparison map and Relation to topological K-theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comparison+map+between+algebraic+and+topological+K-theory"} comparison map between algebraic and topological K-theory
          %h3#Descent Descent
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Moritz Kerz, Florian Strunk,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Tamme"} Georg Tamme
              %em Algebraic K-theory and descent for blow-ups
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.08466"}> arXiv:1611.08466
              )
          %h4#zariski_and_nisnevich_descent Zariski and Nisnevich descent
          %p
            The algebraic K-theory spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{K}
            satisfies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            to give a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+spectra"} connective spectra
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+site"} Zariski site
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BrownGersten73"} Brown Gersten 73
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ThomasonTrobaugh90"} Thomason-Trobaugh 90
          %p
            Moreover,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{K}
            satisfies descent with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nisnevich+site"} Nisnevich topology
            (which lies between Zariski and étale). This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Nisnevich89"}> Nisnevich 89
            ) and was generalized in turn to finite dimensional noetherian schemes in the same paper of Thomason.
          %p
            Further generalization of the descent result to finite dimensional quasi-compact quasi-separated schemes is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Rosenschon06"}> Rosenschon 06
            ).
          %h4#etale_descent Etale descent
          %p
            The question of descent of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{K}
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+site"} étale site
            is closely related to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lichtenbaum-Quillen+conjecture"} Lichtenbaum-Quillen conjecture
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Thomason85"} Thomason 85
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/180265/381"} MO comment
          %h4#AsTheKTheoryOfAlgebraicVectorBundles Description of the K-theory sheaf via algebraic vector bundles
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sch
            denote the gros
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+site"} Zariski site
            of regular, separated, noetherian schemes. It is explained in (
            %a{:href => "#BunkeTamme12"} Bunke-Tamme 12, section 3.3
            that the presheaf of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sch
            defined by algebraic K-theory admits the following description.
          %p
            Regard the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Vect
                    %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Vect}^\oplus
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+vector+bundles"} algebraic vector bundles
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sch
            as taking values in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of vector bundles. Then apply the
            = succeed "-construction" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+of+a+symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} K-theory of a symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            to this, yielding a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+of+spectra"} sheaf of spectra
          %ol
            %li
              %p Zariski descent for Thomason-Trobaugh K-theory,
            %li
              %p the Zariski-local equivalence between Thomason-Trobaugh K-theory, Quillen K-theory, and direct sum K-theory.
